> * compiler verifies correctness (vs. whatever you write in `DebuggerDisplay` is stringly typed)

Current preview can have compiler verification, tho:
```cs
[DebuggerDisplay($"{{{nameof(DebugDisplay)},nq}}")]
public class C
{
    private string DebugDisplay => ...;
}
```

I'm tempted to just reuse the implementation for `KeyValuePair.ToString()`. It's available as a static internal:

```c#
        internal static string PairToString(object? key, object? value) =>
            string.Create(null, stackalloc char[256], $"[{key}, {value}]");
```

There's no escaping or anything else fancy here, as anything like that would have performance hits for negligible value (only in odd cases). Here are the tests I have illustrating what the behavior would be:

```c#
    public class DictionaryEntry_Tests
    {
        public static IEnumerable<object?[]> ToString_Inputs()
        {
            yield return new object?[] { "key", "value", "[key, value]" };
            yield return new object?[] { 1, 2, "[1, 2]" };
            yield return new object?[] { typeof(object), (object?)null, "[System.Object, ]" };
            yield return new object?[] { "[key, value", "]]", "[[key, value, ]]]" };
            yield return new object?[] { new DictionaryEntry("key", "key"), new DictionaryEntry("value", "value"), "[[key, key], [value, value]]" };
        }

        [Theory]
        [MemberData(nameof(ToString_Inputs))]
        public void ToString_FormatsKeyValue(object key, object? value, string expected)
        {
            var entry = new DictionaryEntry(key, value);
            var result = entry.ToString();

            Assert.Equal(expected, result);
        }
    }
```

Any objections to taking this approach? Preview: https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/compare/main...jeffhandley:jeffhandley/59292-de-tostring?expand=1
